{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-163095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=a+b{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2(1+2{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2(1+2{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(0)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1{x}^{2}+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+1=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=2{e}^{x-y},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2+ax-2y+b{x}^{2}+cxy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =2, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+2\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+2x-2y+1{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=2+1+(-2)=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163089" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163089" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163089" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+a{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+(-4)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2}+2xy+(-2){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-\\frac{1}{2})+2+(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{xy},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=a+bxy.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =(1+xy){e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=1+1=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-2y),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ay+b{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-2y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-2y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-2y)-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-2y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-2y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-2y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+0\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(0)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(-2)y+1{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=-2+1=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & -x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y) & x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n0+(-1)+(x-1)+(x-1)+2+(x-1)=3x-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x"] ["math" "2x-1"] ["math" "3x+1"] ["math" "3x+4"] ["math" "3x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{3x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3x-y} & -{e}^{3x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,2) & =[\\begin{aligned}3e & -e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-y} & -3{e}^{3x-y} \\\\ -3{e}^{3x-y} & {e}^{3x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,2) & =[\\begin{aligned}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈e+[\\begin{aligned}3e & -e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\ne+3e+(y-2)+(y-2)+9e+(-3e)=2y+10e-4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2y+10e-4"] ["math" "2y+6"] ["math" "2y+6e-2"] ["math" "x+y+10e"] ["math" "x+y-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163144" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2y+10e-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & -x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x"] ["math" "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "2x-\\pi "] ["math" "x-2\\pi "] ["math" "x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+y}+2x & \\frac{1}{x+y}+3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+2 & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+6y\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+4+(y-1)+(y-1)+5+(y-1)=3y+7.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-163143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2y+8"] ["math" "2y-2"] ["math" "3y+10"] ["math" "3y+7"] ["math" "3y-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-163143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3y+7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-162989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x+2y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-162989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2y} & 2{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2y} & 2{e}^{x+2y} \\\\ 2{e}^{x+2y} & 4{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+2+x+x+2+x=3x+5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-162989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-162989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-162989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+3"] ["math" "2x-3"] ["math" "3x+2"] ["math" "3x+5"] ["math" "3x-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-162989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x+5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =2, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =2, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & ≈2+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-164827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,1) & =1, & \\,{f}^{′}(0,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(0,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,1) & ≈1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2}(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-164858" "field" "