{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =5, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2) & ≈5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+6+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =11+\\frac{1}{2}(44) \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-164831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "26"] ["math" "31"] ["math" "33"] ["math" "52"] ["math" "55"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "33"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & ≈-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-e+2e+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}e \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =e+\\frac{1}{2}(-e+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-164828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3e+1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3e-1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{e+1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{e-1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{e-2}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e+1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(0,2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2,0) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-5+\\frac{1}{2}(-6) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d8cd7641fadd0dd3437d4b7a067e1263a5834f185b3b3fcd08fb3795c52afccd"} {"question" "q-164859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-11"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "q-164859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/state.json" "file_sha256" "0125be5a8684c625f0440d3d03826c62438ba4a70f5ef0a1602c1dc6c8fa12cb"} {"question" "e-12562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables \n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/example-12562.json" "file_sha256" "2fa9e90d7a2200420141f387377b24cdafb2cc28d593299102a512b77eeaaa60"} {"question" "e-12562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/example-12562.json" "file_sha256" "2fa9e90d7a2200420141f387377b24cdafb2cc28d593299102a512b77eeaaa60"} {"question" "e-12563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/b799b1e1fbe7819de86c131989530033d750669ddc8fee88c711b537dad982a6.png) Give your answer in matrix form." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/example-12563.json" "file_sha256" "0909ba6b5eada3168c6bed852c669fdbb49f5cdaff4bc9a8bc65648a49f390d9"} {"question" "e-12563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/b1c2d4408c64daf16466d81c1ea97bdd32f821deb858cd09bc539fc53ebf3ad8.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/c54318fa746f53209585695e2c40d80d793d2b35327b257426b4e4fa7b25463b.png)\n\nand\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/952dafd201f20f1da5b0ac77527beeb4f6d7c259813dbaee74a8d6796dd5d3b2.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/fc105c91cf998f6b564570697321cfb72ac30f81e88dd6c1a0decfe8495259ba.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/example-12563.json" "file_sha256" "0909ba6b5eada3168c6bed852c669fdbb49f5cdaff4bc9a8bc65648a49f390d9"} {"question" "e-12564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/32af7a7ab867c247eff15583688e31052e2d640b1be6c6c1b1f8bd19b7b7f2f7.png) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/example-12564.json" "file_sha256" "77d7af1aea8dd854cfafa8fdf6921f00af047b6baf9dc628f8576121f98e4464"} {"question" "e-12564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/f27bcd29776525aa543b778627e0aace4e0b0537ceac83f5750a31f5def4f762.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/c281563d780c34ea5753498e0e722bfb0296fe06c5d43e82473a45901a1aa7ee.png)\n\nand\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/2fa9abbaad8230a38e1085b8842fbbd970c66addff6f388e13973ee116f0cd37.png)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/71175744ceb56efc01b8b18b97d6681b11b8e9b85a1d899fc9a137ab5e3c611c.png) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/0d3a22cd82a02ab5122e811236d53eb4c0018e9817d2542163acc005ca5d3764.png)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/dad24b303457c56dc14c11b3b9de6cb827b18b8f3a03059922d1b15521ea119d.png) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/example-12564.json" "file_sha256" "77d7af1aea8dd854cfafa8fdf6921f00af047b6baf9dc628f8576121f98e4464"}] "usage" {"e-12563" [] "e-12562" [] "q-162989" [] "q-163113" [] "e-12564" []} "basis" 2463 "retractions" [[17592186198582 :question/problem "For the function of two variables\n\n$f(x, y) = \\cos (x - 2y)$,\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0, 0)$ is given by\n$f(x, y) = 1 + ax^{2} + bxy + cy^{2}$.\nFind the value of $a + b + c$."] [17592186198570 :question/problem "For the function $f: R^{2} → R$, defined as\n\n$f(x, y) = e^{x + 2y}$,\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$f(x) \\approx ∗ + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions."] [17592186198004 :question/problem "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables\n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0)$."] [17592186198004 :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (0,0)\n$$\n\nthen the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx f(0, 0) + f_{x}(0, 0) \\cdot x + f_{y}(0, 0) \\cdot y^(⏞)^(Linear approximation); \\approx + \\frac{1}{2}(f_{xx}(0, 0) \\cdot x^{2} + 2f_{xy}(0, 0) \\cdot xy + f_{yy}(0, 0) \\cdot y^{2})_(⏟)_(Quadratic part)\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n$f(0, 0)|= 1$.\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f_{x}(x, y)|=-\\sin (x + y), & f_{y}(x, y)|=-\\sin (x + y) \\\\ f_{x}(0, 0)|= 0, & f_{y}(0, 0)|= 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f_{xx}(x, y)|=-\\cos (x + y), & f_{xx}(0, 0)|=-1 \\\\ f_{xy}(x, y)|=-\\cos (x + y), & f_{xy}(0, 0)|=-1 \\\\ f_{yy}(x, y)|=-\\cos (x + y), & f_{yy}(0, 0)|=-1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx 1 + 0 \\cdot x + 0 \\cdot y + \\frac{1}{2}((-1) \\cdot x^{2} + 2(-1) \\cdot xy + (-1) \\cdot y^{2}); = 1 - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} - xy - \\frac{1}{2}y^{2}\n$$"] [17592186198005 :question/problem "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, defined as\n\n$f\\left(x,y \\right) = x^2y-xy^3$,\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Give your answer in matrix form."] [17592186198005 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand is twice differentiable at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nThen, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\mid \\approx f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}f^{″}(a)(x - a)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, \\mid f^{″}(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n$f(1, 1)|= 0$.\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n$f^{′}(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2xy - y^{3} & x^{2} - 3xy^{2}\\end{bmatrix}; f^{′}(1, 1)|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n$f^{″}(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x - 3y^{2} \\\\ 2x - 3y^{2} & -6xy\\end{bmatrix}; f^{″}(1, 1)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186198006 :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$, we have the following data:\n\n$f(1, 0, 0)|=-1, \\mid f^{′}(1, 0, 0)|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\mid f^{″}(1, 0, 0)|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1)$."] [17592186198006 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand is twice differentiable at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nThen, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\mid \\approx f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}f^{″}(a)(x - a)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(a) &= -1, \\mid f^{′}(a)|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\mid f^{″}(a)|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(1, 1, 1) \\mid \\approx - 1 + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}; =-1 + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}; =-1 + 1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{2}(3); = \\frac{3}{2}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186194322 17592186194323 17592186194324 17592186194325 17592186194326 17592186194394 17592186194395 17592186194396 17592186194397 17592186194398] "immutable_fields" [[17592186194321 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194321 :answer-field/id] [17592186194321 :answer-field/key] [17592186194321 :answer-field/type] [17592186194393 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194393 :answer-field/id] [17592186194393 :answer-field/key] [17592186194393 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
